<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Maranao people</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Maranaos</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Maranao are a Muslim people of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a> whose homeland is the region around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-lanao">Lake Lanao</a> on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mindanao">Mindanao</a>; their name means 'people of the lake'. They accepted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> from the 16th century and organised themselves into many small sultanates that long resisted Spanish and American rule. The Maranao are known for brass work, weaving, the okir style of decorative carving and the epic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darangen">Darangen</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/maranao-people.jpg" alt="Maranao people" width="320" height="456" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: วัศย์รุจ ปริญญาวุฒิชัย · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Maranao_performing_Singkil.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The ancestral group is believed to have called itself Iranaoan. It later diverged: the Maguindanaon and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iranun">Iranun people</a>, whose names can also be translated as people of the lake, moved away, while those who stayed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-lanao">Lake Lanao</a> became the Maranao. The three groups remain related, share similar cultures and speak languages of the Danao family, which also gave its name to the island of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mindanao">Mindanao</a>. Lake Lanao, the largest lake in Mindanao and the second-largest and deepest in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a>, is the centre of Maranao life and the subject of many myths and legends.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Maranao were the last of the Muslims of the southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a> to adopt <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>, chiefly under the influence of Arab-Malay missionaries such as Sharif Kabungsuwan and others from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borneo">Borneo</a>. Before Islam their leaders were tribal chiefs called datu; with its arrival in the 16th century they formed kingdoms ruled by sultans. Through the nominal Spanish occupation of the Philippines, and later the American and Japanese, they kept their own leaders. Amai Pakpak led a resistance against Spanish colonisation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lanao">Lanao</a> and is honoured as a national hero, and in the 20th century Rashid Lucman, a journalist and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">World War II</a> guerrilla, was an early proponent of Moro independence or autonomy.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Traditional society is divided into two strata: the mapiyatao, of pure descent and entitled to ascend to thrones by royal bloodline, and the kasilidan, suspected of mixed descent, who are further divided into categories that include non-Maranao and slaves. These distinctions have begun to decline as economic conditions have improved. The Maranao have long lived by fishing and farming, growing wet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a>, corn and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweet-potato">sweet potatoes</a>, and their textiles and woodcarvings are sold throughout the country. They numbered about 1,800,000 in 2020, living mainly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lanao">Lanao</a> del Norte and Lanao del Sur.</p><p>Houses stand on piles in three tiers of rank: the torogan of royalty, the mala a walay of the lesser nobility and the lawig of commoners. The Sarimanok, a bird of Maranao myth holding a fish and decorated with okir scrolls, is a symbol of good fortune found throughout their art. Kulintang gong music, the Singkil dance, based on a scene of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darangen">Darangen</a>, and the Maharadia Lawana, a version of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramayana">Ramayana</a>, are part of their heritage, and in 2005 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> selected the Darangen as a Masterpiece of the Oral and Intangible Heritage of Humanity.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Maranao people” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Islam reached the <b>Maranao people</b> of the Lake Lanao plateau from the Maguindanao Sultanate and the Sulu coast in the 16th and 17th centuries.</blockquote><a href="/state/lanao-sultanates">Lanao sultanates</a></li></ul></section>
